**ASSESSMENT & RECOMMENDATIONS**

{{DIAGNOSTIC SPECIFICITY & CODING RULES — apply to every diagnosis in this section:
Group related diagnoses together. Make every diagnosis as specific as possible with laterality when available. Do NOT write ICD codes anywhere in the note.
- Acute Encephalopathy: always specify etiology — 'due to [cause]'
- Malnutrition: detect when criteria met; specify mild / moderate / severe in title
- Sepsis: always specify organism and/or source in title
- Type 2 Myocardial Infarction: always spell out in full — never abbreviate as 'Type 2 MI'
- AKI: always specify with or without ATN/vasomotor nephropathy in title
- SIRS: list as a separate active problem; title specifies with or without organ dysfunction
- Antibiotics: specify start date and projected duration (do not reference guideline names)
- Catheter-associated infection: title specifies catheter-associated or not
- GI/any bleeding: title specifies if due to or associated with anticoagulant; name the anticoagulant only if it was in use prior to the bleeding}}

{{A&P FORMAT (HARDWIRED): BOLD TITLE + DASH LINES ONLY. Each problem is EXACTLY: one BOLD title line — the literal character '#' immediately followed by the diagnosis with NO space after '#', bolded with '**' only, exact form '**#Acute blood loss anemia, improving post transfusion [POA]**' — no '####' or '###' heading marks on the title — then, on the IMMEDIATELY following line with NO blank line in between, the dash lines: each item on its own line starting with the literal three characters '\- ' (backslash, hyphen, space). The backslash is REQUIRED — it stops the chat interface from turning the line into a '•' bullet glyph, so the reader sees and copies a literal '-'. Never begin an item with unescaped '- ', '•', '*', numbered lists, or indentation. No blank lines between dash lines within a problem. Between problems: exactly ONE blank line before the next bold title (and one blank line before the '**#Chronic Problems**' header). Do NOT use '&nbsp;', markdown heading marks, or horizontal rules as separators. No section-reset lines, anchors, or rules between problems. Titles carry NO certainty words (likely/possible/suspected/confirmed); a brief factual trajectory phrase explicitly supported by the data is allowed. NO 'Assessment:' label or paragraph. NO 'Dx & Consults' or 'Plan' subheaders. NO narrative sentences outside dash lines. NO empty or placeholder bullets. Evidence items first (labs/imaging/consults for this problem, exact values and dates as provided), then action items (meds with dose/route/frequency exactly as provided, antibiotics with start date and projected duration, and monitoring with parameter/frequency/threshold). Do NOT add hypothetical 'If X, then Y' contingency or advisory lines — document only current, actual orders and monitoring unless a contingency plan is explicitly stated in the provided data (the Two-Midnight status statement and the C. diff prophylaxis statement are exempt where their locks require them). The FIRST problem MUST be the main admitting diagnosis, and when the Two-Midnight gating conditions are met its FINAL dash line is the status statement per TWO_MIDNIGHT_STATEMENT_MAIN_DX_LOCK. Minimum 4 dash lines per problem when data supports them. Only write 'dose/route/frequency not provided' when those details are truly absent from the input.}}

{{FORMAT & DEPTH EXAMPLE — BEGIN. This block defines the REQUIRED structure and level of detail for EVERY problem block. It is a formatting example ONLY. NEVER copy its diagnoses, values, medications, consultant names, or dates into the note; all real content comes exclusively from the provided input data.

**#Sepsis due to Escherichia coli urinary tract infection [POA]**
\- Blood cultures (07/01): 2/2 bottles E. coli, pan-sensitive — confirms urinary source.
\- CT abdomen/pelvis (07/01): no perinephric abscess or obstruction.
\- Lactate 4.1 mmol/L on presentation with WBC 18.4.
\- Start ceftriaxone 2 g IV every 24 hours, started 07/01, projected 7-day course through 07/07.
\- Obtain repeat lactate and daily CBC, BMP; follow blood culture sensitivities.
\- Maintain IV fluid resuscitation per protocol; monitor urine output and hemodynamics.
\- Based on the patient's current clinical condition, comorbidities, severity of illness, and need for hospital-level monitoring and treatment for management of Sepsis due to Escherichia coli urinary tract infection, I anticipate the patient will require medically necessary inpatient care spanning at least two midnights.

**#Acute kidney injury without ATN [POA]**
\- Creatinine 1.9 on admission from baseline 1.0.
\- Urine output adequate.
\- Continue IV fluids and monitor daily BMP.
\- Hold nephrotoxic agents; renally dose medications.

FORMAT & DEPTH EXAMPLE — END. Every real problem block below MUST match this structure and depth: bold '#' title, then dash lines starting on the immediately following line, nothing else. This example governs ONLY the ASSESSMENT & RECOMMENDATIONS section — every other section of the note (Chief Complaint, HPI, PMH, PSH, ROS, Social, Family, Allergies, Home Medications, Active Medications, Objective, Physical Exam, Labs & Diagnostics, Chronic Problems, DVT/Code/Disposition, time statement, disclaimer) MUST still be rendered in full per FULL_NOTE_ALL_SECTIONS_REQUIRED_LOCK.}}

**#[Dx Title] [POA if the diagnosis was present on admission, otherwise leave empty]**
\- {{evidence dash lines first, starting on the line IMMEDIATELY after the title with no blank line between, each line beginning with the literal characters '\- ': each relevant lab/imaging/ECG/micro result or consult explicitly provided, one per bullet, exact values and dates, brief tie to this problem; consultants as 'Specialty – Dr. Name (MM/DD/YYYY): one-line recommendation'. If none provided for this problem, start directly with action bullets.}}
\- {{action bullets, each line beginning with the literal characters '\- ': meds with name + dose + route + frequency exactly as provided (antibiotics with start date + projected duration); monitoring with parameter, frequency, and action threshold; pending/planned diagnostics. NO hypothetical 'If X, then Y' contingency or advisory lines. Each bullet starts with an action verb. Minimum 4 dash lines total per problem when data supports them. For the FIRST (main) problem ONLY: when gating conditions are met, the FINAL dash line is the Two-Midnight status statement naming the main diagnosis exactly as written in its title.}}

{{Repeat the above diagnosis block for ALL active inpatient problems. Separate consecutive problems with exactly ONE blank line — no '&nbsp;', no section-reset lines, no anchors, no horizontal rules between problems.}}

**#Chronic Problems**

**#[Chronic Dx Title]**
\- {{dash lines in the SAME format as active problems, each beginning with the literal characters '\- ': current status supported by provided data; home/continued medications with name + dose + route + frequency exactly as provided; monitoring or inpatient considerations relevant this admission. Use as many dash lines as the data supports (2 or more when available).}}

{{Repeat the chronic problem block for ALL stable chronic problems, separated by exactly one blank line, same bold-title + escaped-dash format as active problems. Stable chronic problems appear ONLY here and are not duplicated among active problems.}}

